## @PreAuthorize Examples

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    // Must have ADMIN or MANAGER role
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or hasRole('MANAGER')")
    public List<Order> getAllOrders() {
        return orderRepo.findAll();
    }

    // User can only access their own orders
    // #userId is the method parameter name
    @PreAuthorize("#userId == authentication.principal.id or hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public List<Order> getOrdersByUser(Long userId) {
        return orderRepo.findByUserId(userId);
    }

    // Access authentication object's custom fields
    @PreAuthorize("@orderSecurityService.canAccess(authentication, #orderId)")
    public Order getOrder(Long orderId) {
        return orderRepo.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
    }

    // Check on method argument (complex object)
    @PreAuthorize("#request.userId == authentication.principal.id")
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest request) {
        return orderRepo.save(new Order(request));
    }
}
```

## @PostAuthorize — Check Return Value

```java
// Verify the returned object belongs to the current user
@PostAuthorize("returnObject.userId == authentication.principal.id or hasRole('ADMIN')")
public Order findById(Long id) {
    return orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // Method runs, THEN access check happens on the returned Order
    // If check fails: AccessDeniedException, but DB query already ran
}
// Use for cases where the ownership check requires loading the object first
```

## Custom Security Bean for Complex Logic

```java
@Component("orderSecurity")  // Bean name used in SpEL
public class OrderSecurityService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;

    public boolean canAccess(Authentication auth, Long orderId) {
        if (auth.getAuthorities().stream()
                .anyMatch(a -> a.getAuthority().equals("ROLE_ADMIN"))) {
            return true;  // Admin can access anything
        }
        CustomUserDetails user = (CustomUserDetails) auth.getPrincipal();
        return orderRepo.existsByIdAndUserId(orderId, user.getUserId());
    }
}

// Usage:
@PreAuthorize("@orderSecurity.canAccess(authentication, #orderId)")
public Order getOrder(Long orderId) { ... }
```

## @PreFilter and @PostFilter

```java
// Filter input collection — only process orders owned by current user
@PreFilter("filterObject.userId == authentication.principal.id")
public List<Order> processOrders(List<Order> orders) {
    // orders is filtered before method executes
    return orders.stream().map(this::process).collect(toList());
}

// Filter return collection
@PostFilter("filterObject.userId == authentication.principal.id or hasRole('ADMIN')")
public List<Order> getAllOrdersFiltered() {
    return orderRepo.findAll();  // All loaded, then non-owned filtered out
    // Note: inefficient for large datasets — better to filter in query
}
```

## Production Reality

Method security is excellent for enforcing resource ownership rules without boilerplate checks in every method. However, `@PostFilter` and `@PostAuthorize` can be expensive — they load data from the DB first, then discard it if access is denied. Where possible, enforce ownership in the query itself (e.g., `findByIdAndUserId`) to avoid unnecessary data loading. Use method security for the ownership check layer, but let the repository filter for efficiency.

## BCryptPasswordEncoder

```java
@Bean
public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12);  // Strength 12 (rounds = 2^12 = 4096)
    // Strength 10 = ~100ms, 12 = ~400ms, 14 = ~1600ms
    // Higher strength = slower = harder to brute force
}

// Encoding:
String rawPassword = "myPassword123";
String encoded = passwordEncoder.encode(rawPassword);
// encoded = "$2a$12$xH9bX8VxB2RkSVkz..." (includes salt + cost)

// Verification:
boolean matches = passwordEncoder.matches(rawPassword, encoded);
// matches = true
// Note: encode() same password twice produces DIFFERENT hashes (different salts)
// matches() is the only way to verify

// Registration:
@PostMapping("/register")
public ResponseEntity<?> register(@RequestBody RegisterRequest req) {
    if (userRepo.existsByEmail(req.getEmail())) {
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Email already taken");
    }
    User user = new User();
    user.setEmail(req.getEmail());
    user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode(req.getPassword()));
    userRepo.save(user);
    return ResponseEntity.ok().build();
}
```

## DelegatingPasswordEncoder (Recommended for Production)

```java
@Bean
public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    // Creates DelegatingPasswordEncoder with bcrypt as default
    return PasswordEncoderFactories.createDelegatingPasswordEncoder();
}

// Stores passwords with prefix:
// {bcrypt}$2a$10$...
// {argon2}...
// {noop}plaintext  (dev only!)

// Benefit: can migrate from one algorithm to another
// Old passwords: {bcrypt}... still verified correctly
// New passwords: use updated default algorithm
// Migration: re-hash on successful login with new algorithm

// Manual migration on login:
@Override
public Authentication authenticate(Authentication auth) throws AuthenticationException {
    User user = userRepo.findByEmail((String) auth.getPrincipal()).orElseThrow(...);
    if (!passwordEncoder.matches((String) auth.getCredentials(), user.getPassword())) {
        throw new BadCredentialsException("Bad credentials");
    }
    // Re-encode with current algorithm if outdated
    if (passwordEncoder.upgradeEncoding(user.getPassword())) {
        user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode((String) auth.getCredentials()));
        userRepo.save(user);
    }
    return new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(user, null, getAuthorities(user));
}
```

## Password Reset Best Practices

```java
@Service
public class PasswordResetService {

    public void initiateReset(String email) {
        User user = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("User not found"));
            // Always return 200 even if email not found — prevent email enumeration!

        String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        PasswordResetToken resetToken = new PasswordResetToken();
        resetToken.setToken(passwordEncoder.encode(token));  // Hash the reset token too!
        resetToken.setUser(user);
        resetToken.setExpiresAt(LocalDateTime.now().plusHours(1));
        resetTokenRepo.save(resetToken);

        emailService.sendPasswordResetEmail(email, token);  // Send plain token, store hash
    }

    public void resetPassword(String token, String newPassword) {
        // Find candidate tokens, verify against stored hashes
        // Invalidate after use
        // Apply new password:
        user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode(newPassword));
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

BCrypt strength 12 is the current sweet spot — ~400ms per hash on modern hardware is safe from brute force while imperceptible to users (login is a one-time operation). Argon2 is the modern alternative (winner of the Password Hashing Competition) — Spring Security 5.8+ includes `Argon2PasswordEncoder`. Either is appropriate; BCrypt is more battle-tested in Java.

## Persistent Remember-Me (Recommended)

```java
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Autowired
    private DataSource dataSource;

    @Bean
    public PersistentTokenRepository persistentTokenRepository() {
        JdbcTokenRepositoryImpl tokenRepository = new JdbcTokenRepositoryImpl();
        tokenRepository.setDataSource(dataSource);
        tokenRepository.setCreateTableOnStartup(false);  // Use Flyway migration
        return tokenRepository;
    }

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .rememberMe(remember -> remember
                .tokenRepository(persistentTokenRepository())
                .tokenValiditySeconds(7 * 24 * 60 * 60)  // 7 days
                .rememberMeParameter("remember-me")       // Form field name
                .key("uniqueAndSecretKey")                // HMAC key
            );
        return http.build();
    }
}

// DB table (create via Flyway):
/*
CREATE TABLE persistent_logins (
    username varchar(64) NOT NULL,
    series varchar(64) PRIMARY KEY,
    token varchar(64) NOT NULL,
    last_used timestamp NOT NULL
);
*/
```

## How Persistent Tokens Work

```
First login with "Remember Me" checked:
  Server: series = random UUID, token = random UUID
  Server: store {series, token, username} in DB
  Client: Set-Cookie: remember-me=series:token (7 days)

Return visit (remember-me cookie present):
  Server: find {series} in DB → get stored token
  Server: compare stored token with cookie token
  Server: match → authenticate, generate NEW token for same series
  Server: update DB: {series, newToken, username}
  Client: Set-Cookie: remember-me=series:newToken (refreshed)

Stealth token theft detection:
  Legitimate user: uses series with token1 → gets token2
  Attacker (who stole token1): tries to use token1 again
  Server: series exists but token1 != token2
  → THEFT DETECTED: delete all tokens for this series
  → Both legitimate user and attacker are logged out
  → Alert user to potential compromise
```

## Production Reality

For web applications: use persistent remember-me with a 7-30 day validity. For REST APIs: use refresh tokens instead — they're more flexible (revocable, scope-limited, detectable theft). Remember-me is a significant security surface — consider requiring password re-entry for sensitive operations even when remember-me is active (step-up authentication).
